<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened Runtime entitlements for the BulkSeq Studio.app bundle, applied by
  scripts/build_macos.sh via "codesign (deep, options runtime, entitlements
  packaging/entitlements.plist) ...".

  WHY EACH KEY IS HERE (QtWebEngine, used by the PPI tab, is the constraint;
  it embeds Chromium, which needs a JIT and loads its own unsigned-at-rest
  helper libraries). NOTE: XML comments cannot contain a double hyphen, so
  flag names below are written without the leading dashes.

  - com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit
      Chromium's V8 JavaScript engine needs to allocate W^X executable memory
      for JIT-compiled code. Required on Apple Silicon whenever a bundled
      process runs a JIT engine. Confirmed applicable to QtWebEngineProcess by
      the Qt Forum thread below; entitlement reference:
      https://developer.apple.com/documentation/bundleresources/entitlements/com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit

  - com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
      Chromium/V8 also maps executable pages that are not backed by a signed
      file on disk (JIT output, and some of its own generated code). Hardened
      Runtime blocks this by default. Reported as required for
      QtWebEngineProcess under a hardened-runtime codesign pass:
      https://forum.qt.io/topic/151688/codesign-unable-to-sign-qtwebengineprocess-with-options-runtime
      https://developer.apple.com/documentation/bundleresources/entitlements/com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory

  - com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
      By default Hardened Runtime only loads libraries signed by the same Team
      ID as the main executable. A PyInstaller onedir bundle ships Qt/Chromium
      shared libraries signed by their own upstream certificates (or, for some
      PySide6 wheel-provided .so/.dylib files, not signed at all), so without
      this entitlement the frozen app fails to load them at runtime. Same
      requirement reported for embedded-Chromium apps generally (Firefox/Gecko
      hit the identical constraint):
      https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1570840
      https://developer.apple.com/documentation/bundleresources/entitlements/com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation

  DELIBERATELY NOT SET: com.apple.security.app-sandbox. Qt's own platform
  notes state that App Sandbox "interferes with Chromium's own sandbox
  initialization, which results in Chromium not being properly initialized":
  https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qtwebengine-platform-notes.html
  Do not add this key.

  UNVERIFIED (deliberately left out, not guessed in): a Qt Forum reply in the
  same thread above mentions com.apple.security.cs.disable-executable-page-protection
  as possibly needed for QtWebEngineProcess specifically ("may be needed"), and
  some Chromium-embedding apps also carry
  com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables. Neither was confirmed
  necessary for a PyInstaller-frozen PySide6 app in the sources checked, and
  the PyInstaller onedir mode plus PySide6 plus QtWebEngine notarization path
  has open, unresolved reports upstream:
  https://github.com/pyinstaller/pyinstaller/issues/8927
  If notarization or first-run fails with a JIT/library-load crash inside
  QtWebEngineProcess after a real signing pass on macOS hardware, add
  disable-executable-page-protection here next and re-verify on-device; this
  cannot be confirmed from Windows.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
